summary_en.experiments:
From the provided full-text excerpt, the experiments cover mainstream open LLM families including LLaMA and Qwen, and compare against methods such as SingleQuant, DARTQuant, FlatQuant, and OSTQuant. The main empirical claim is that TORQ preserves model quality more reliably under MXFP4 activation quantization, especially in layers where heavy-tailed activations make shared-exponent quantization particularly fragile.

The most informative results are likely not just end-task scores, but the intermediate diagnostics that support the paper’s mechanism:
1. whether block variance histograms become flatter after the macro rotation;
2. whether the fraction of small-magnitude values quantized to zero drops materially;
3. whether MXFP4 codebook occupancy becomes more uniform and codebook collapse is alleviated;
4. how much gain comes from macro-only, micro-only, and the full two-level method.

The introduction already gives two important quantitative observations that motivate the method: more than 90% of small-magnitude elements in a block can be zeroed out when the shared exponent is driven up, and roughly 50% of the codeword range is activated by only 10% of the data during real inference. Those numbers are central to the paper’s argument. When reading the full paper, it is worth checking the exact perplexity or downstream accuracy gaps in the main tables, as well as robustness across block sizes, calibration-set sizes, and model scales.
